@loom 1.0
!must keep public webhook signature verification path unchanged because external consumers depend on it
!never rotate Stripe API keys during migration window
!never any downtime for checkout flow
!must keep feature flag billing.new_client defaulting to off
!must Test suite pass before we merge anything
!must All existing webhook consumers continue to receive events with same shape they receive today
#evidence{id,trust,text}
  e1,,"Test failed was test_webhook_signature_validation and error message came back was ""expected 200 but got 401""."
  e2,,Configuration file is config.py and on line 88 it sets webhook tolerance to 300 s.
  e3,,Staging clock was measured to be about 42 s ahead of auth server.
  e4,,"This is interesting because it is well within 300 s tolerance, so on face of it clock skew should not be cause, but I am not completely certain about yet."
  e5,,test_webhook_signature_validation ... FAILED expected: 200 actual: 401 file: tests/test_webhooks.py:41
  e6,,"So plan I would suggest is we first confirm which time source signature verification path uses, then add regression test simulates clock skew of 42 s, then re-run whole webhook suite in staging, and only after should we consider changing anything in production."
#notes
  - This is value is checked into repository, and I have not verified whether staging or production override it with environment variable, so is something we should check.
  - One more important thing to note is signature verification path uses different time source than rest of request handling, and I believe this is root cause but I have not proven it yet.
  - We need answers to all three before we can be confident about fix.
?q Does production use NTP?
?q Is webhook tolerance overridable from environment?
?q Does signature verification path read time source at import time or at request time?
